<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peel Commission</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1936">1936</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Palestine Royal Commission</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peel Commission was a British royal commission led by Lord Peel, appointed in 1936 to investigate the causes of the Arab revolt in Mandatory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. Its report of July 1937 concluded that the mandate was unworkable and proposed, for the first time, partition into a Jewish state and an Arab state, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and a corridor to the sea. Arab leaders rejected the plan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peel-commission.jpg" alt="Peel Commission" width="320" height="767" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: UK Government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PeelMap.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The commission was set up after a six-month Arab general strike, at a time of serious clashes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> that began in 1936 and lasted three years. Its chairman was William Peel, 1st Earl Peel, its vice-chairman Sir Horace Rumbold, and its other members Sir Laurie Hammond, Sir Morris Carter, Sir Harold Morris and Reginald Coupland. It arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> on 11 November 1936. Chaim Weizmann spoke for the Jews, telling it on 25 November of 6,000,000 Jews in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for whom the world was divided into places where they could not live and places they could not enter. The Mufti of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, Hajj Amin al-Husseini, testified against any partition and demanded an end to Jewish immigration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The commission judged the causes of the revolt to be the desire of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> for national independence and their hostility to the Jewish National Home, sharpened by fear of Jewish domination. It concluded that the two communities' national aspirations were incompatible and the mandate unworkable. Its plan gave the Jewish state the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jezreel-valley">Jezreel Valley</a> and the coast from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-carmel">Mount Carmel</a> to south of Be'er Tuvia, joined the rest, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negev">Negev</a>, to Trans-Jordan, and proposed a transfer of land and an exchange of population on the model of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> in 1923, involving up to 225,000 Arabs and 1,250 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, compulsory in the last resort.</p><p>The Arab Higher Committee rejected partition and called for an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> with protection of minority rights. The Nashashibi faction and Emir Abdullah of Trans-Jordan had first supported it, but the Nashashibis published their rejection two days before the committee, and the Bloudan Conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> repudiated the plan in September 1937. The Twentieth Zionist Congress rejected the proposed borders in August 1937 but empowered its executive to negotiate a better plan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Peel plan became the master partition plan to which later ones were compared. In March 1938 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> appointed the Woodhead Commission to work out an actual partition; it rejected the Peel plan because it could not be carried out without a massive forced transfer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, and the government then rejected partition as impracticable. In 1981 it was disclosed that the Jewish Agency had placed microphones in the commission's meeting room.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peel Commission” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Arabs rejected the <b>Peel Commission</b>'s partition the revolt resumed in 1937; the British lost Nablus and Hebron for a time, then crushed it with overwhelming force, 6,000 armed Jewish auxiliary police and Orde Wingate's Special Night Squads raiding Arab villages, while the Irgun attacked Arab marketplaces…</blockquote><a href="/empire/mandatory-palestine">Mandatory Palestine · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Anger at land purchases and British rule grew into the Arab Revolt of 1936–1939; the <b>Peel Commission</b> proposed partition in 1937, and the White Paper of 1939 limited Jewish immigration.</blockquote><a href="/movement/zionism">Zionism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
